## Diagnosis

The root cause of all 22 failures is a single control-flow bug in Phase 1 of `main()`. In the option-recognition branch:

```c
if (strcmp(arg, "-p") == 0 || strcmp(arg, "--parents") == 0) {
    use_parents = 1;
} else if (arg[1] == '-') {
    fprintf(stderr, "%s: unrecognized option '%s'\n", PROGNAM, arg);
} else {
    fprintf(stderr, "%s: invalid option -- '%c'\n", PROGNAM, arg[1]);
}
fprintf(stderr, "Try '%s --help' for more information.\n", PROGNAM);
return 1;
```

The `fprintf` + `return 1` are **outside** the `else` branches, so they execute unconditionally — even when `-p` is successfully recognized. This causes every invocation with `-p` to immediately print the help hint and exit 1 before any directory is created.

## Fix

Move the error output and `return 1` inside an `else` block so they only fire for unrecognized options:

